{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>For the human is a tree of the field.</b> I have already explained in the Book of Foundation that it is possible in every language to abbreviate, to take a shortened path, like 'חמור לחם' [bread donkey]. But the word 'לא' cannot be elided, for the meaning would be reversed. A great grammarian of Spain said that the ה'א is missing, and so it is: 'כי האדם עץ השדה' [the ה'א is absent]. But this explanation is not correct in my eyes, for what reason would there be to say, 'Do not destroy a fruit tree,' on the grounds that it is not like human beings who could flee from before you? In my opinion we have no need for any of this. This is its meaning: 'For from it you shall eat, and it you shall not cut down, for the human is a tree of the field.' The sense is: 'For the life of the human is the tree of the field.' Similarly, 'כי נפש הוא חובל' [for he is a wounded soul] means 'for the life of the wounded soul.'" "<b>And it you shall not cut down.</b> It is connected with 'to come before you in siege.' Namely: you shall not destroy a fruit tree, which is life for a human; only you may eat from it, and it is forbidden to you to destroy it in order that 'you may come against the city before you in siege.' The proof that this interpretation is correct is that it says, 'and you shall cut down and build siege-works.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra argues that כי האדם means כי הוא אדם, i.e., כי is functioning with an elided הוא; the Spanish grammarian he disputes appears to hold that the ה'א of the definite article on האדם is missing, yielding כי אדם."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:24:11.233077426Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}